CRYSTALWORLDS.MEDIA PROJECT LICENCE 1.1
=======================================

Version 1.1, 4 November 2026. It replaces version 1.0.

Copyright (c) 2026 the CrystalWorlds.Media project. All rights reserved
except as granted below.

This single licence covers both the project's software and its original
writing and interface art. It replaces the project's earlier split of
PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 (code) and CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (assets), which
together granted a right this project does not intend to grant: the right to
take the work apart.


PLAIN-LANGUAGE SUMMARY
----------------------
The sections below are what legally binds. This summary is here so the intent
is not in doubt.

  YOU MAY, FREELY AND WITHOUT ASKING:
    * run the Project, privately or publicly, for non-commercial purposes
    * study it, and learn from it
    * change it, and publish your changed version of the WHOLE Project
    * share it, whole, changed or unchanged
    * read, build, run and test its source code and its emulator cores to
      check the results it publishes, and publish what you find (section 3B)
    * repeat its clean-room proof, with an AI if you like, only to check its
      results, and publish what you find (section 3D)
    * read, use, cite and reference its research and documentation, use the
      facts it states, with credit, to improve your own projects, and quote
      reasonable excerpts with credit, in paid work too (section 3A)

  ON ONE CONDITION:
    * credit this project, visibly, as set out in section 4

  YOU MAY NOT, WITHOUT ASKING FIRST:
    * take a piece out and use it on its own -- an emulator core, a device
      page, the interface, the artwork pipeline, the harnesses
    * make money from it, in any way
    * call your version "Crystal Worlds", or present it, or yourself, as
      this project (section 4A)
    * copy its research and documentation in its entirety, or substantial
      parts of it, or present any of it as your own work (section 3A)
    * build an emulator, an emulator core or a front end from its research
      and documentation (section 3A)
    * have an AI or any other automated system build software mainly from
      its research, documentation or source code, or train, fine-tune or
      test an AI model on them (section 3C)
    * mine its text or data: that right is reserved (section 3E)
    * anything else this licence does not grant

  YOU MAY NOT, AT ALL:
    * use its source code or its emulator cores (as source or as built
      files), in part or in full, to develop any other front-end software
      (section 3B)

  THE SHORT VERSION OF THE WHOLE THING:
    ** YOU MAY CHANGE IT. YOU MAY NOT SEVER IT. **

  Everything is "please ask" unless this licence says otherwise. The answer is
  often yes. See section 7.

  AND, IMPORTANTLY: parts of this project are other people's work under other
  people's licences, and NOTHING above applies to them. Section 6 names them.
  Where section 6 applies, their licence governs and this one does not.


1. DEFINITIONS
--------------
"The Project" means CrystalWorlds.Media as distributed: the site, its pages,
its emulator cores, its interface, its data, its documentation, its build and
verification tooling, taken together as one work.

"Project Material" means everything in the Project that this project itself
wrote or made, and that is not Exempt Material.

"Exempt Material" means the third-party work listed in section 6, which is
governed by its own licence and not by this one.

"Component" means any separable part of the Project: a source file, a compiled
artefact, a WebAssembly module, a page, a stylesheet, a data file, an image, a
document, a tool, or any identifiable subset of these.

"Source Code" means the Project Material in the form software is made from:
source files, build and check scripts and build notes, wherever the Project
publishes them, including in a repository that holds only the source of its
emulator cores.

"Cores" means the Project's emulators and emulator cores, both as Source Code
and as built files (WebAssembly modules, scripts, and any other compiled or
generated form), including the built files in the Project's client and on its
site.

"Front-End Software" means software through which people find, launch, play,
watch, view or manage games or other media: for example an emulator front
end, a launcher, a game library site or app, a media player, or a play
window. "Other Front-End Software" means any Front-End Software except the
Project itself and a modified version of the whole Project that is
distributed as the Project under section 2(c), in compliance with this
licence. Software that uses the Project, or any part of it, as the base of a
different product is Other Front-End Software.

"Automated System" means any software that produces code, text or other
output from what it is given, including a machine-learning model, an AI
assistant, chatbot or agent, and a code generator.

"Non-commercial" means not primarily intended for or directed toward
commercial advantage or monetary compensation. Being paid for something else
while using the Project does not by itself make your use commercial; charging
for the Project, charging for access to it, bundling it into a paid product or
service, or using it to advertise or promote a paid product or service, does.


2. GRANT
--------
Subject to every condition and limit in this licence, you are granted a
worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive licence to:

  (a) use and run the Project for non-commercial purposes;
  (b) copy and distribute the Project;
  (c) modify the Project and distribute your modified version of it.

Permissions (b) and (c) apply TO THE PROJECT AS A WHOLE. They do not extend
to any Component on its own. See section 3.


3. THE LIMIT -- NO SEVERANCE
----------------------------
You may not extract, isolate, sever, re-package or distribute any Component of
the Project Material separately from the Project, or incorporate any Component
into another work, without prior written permission. Our test-cartridge
downloads (the zips under /downloads) are an exception: each carries its own
MIT licence, in the LICENSE file inside the zip, and that licence governs it.

This is the central restriction of this licence and it is deliberate. The
emulator cores in particular were written here, from an understanding of the
hardware rather than from anyone else's source, and they are not offered as
standalone libraries.

For the avoidance of doubt:

  PERMITTED, no permission needed:
    * forking the whole Project and publishing your changed version of it,
      with credit, non-commercially
    * removing parts you do not want from YOUR copy of the whole Project
    * quoting the documentation, with credit

  NOT PERMITTED without asking:
    * publishing a Component of this Project as a library, package or asset
    * copying this Project's interface or device pages into another site

  NOT PERMITTED AT ALL (section 3B):
    * shipping an emulator core from this Project inside your own application,
      or using one, in part or in full, to develop Other Front-End Software

Modification is welcome. Dismantling is not. If what you want is a part rather
than the whole, section 7 is the route, and it is a real route, except for
what section 3B prohibits.


3A. RESEARCH AND DOCUMENTATION -- CITE IT, DO NOT CLAIM IT
----------------------------------------------------------
"Research and Documentation" means the Project Material that is written
explanation: the Hardware and Emulation pages, the compatibility research and
results, the test write-ups, and the Project's other documentation.

The facts that the Research and Documentation reports belong to no one, and
this licence claims no right over them. What this project owns, and what this
section licenses, is the written work: its wording, its tables and diagrams,
and the way it is selected and arranged.

In addition to section 2, you are granted a worldwide, royalty-free,
non-exclusive licence to:

  (a) read, study and use the Research and Documentation for research,
      writing, teaching and your own understanding, and use the facts and
      findings it states, with credit, to check, correct and improve your
      own existing projects;
  (b) cite it, reference it and link to it;
  (c) quote reasonable excerpts of it in your own work, whether or not that
      work is commercial.

Section 3 does not stop you doing anything this section permits.

QUOTATION IN COMMERCIAL WORK: permission (c) is an exception to section 5,
for quotation only. A book, a paid article, a monetised video or a commercial
product may quote reasonable excerpts, with credit as below. No other
commercial use is granted by this section: copying the Research and
Documentation in its entirety or in substantial parts, presenting it as your
own work, and every other commercial use of the Project Material remain
governed by section 5 and are not permitted without asking.

ON ONE CONDITION: where you quote it under (c), credit the CrystalWorlds.Media
project as the source of the quoted text, next to it or in your references,
with a link where one is possible. Where your work uses a finding you learned
here under (a), credit the project in the same way.

NOT PERMITTED without asking:
  * copying the Research and Documentation in its entirety, or substantial
    parts of it, into another work, whether word for word or lightly
    reworded;
  * presenting any of its wording, tables or diagrams as your own work, or
    removing the credit from what you quote;
  * using the Research and Documentation as the source from which you build
    an emulator, an emulator core or Front-End Software. Permission (a)
    covers improving a project you already have with what you learn here;
    it does not cover building a new one from it.

Copying the Research and Documentation as part of the WHOLE Project, under
section 2, remains permitted as before. Nothing in this section limits what
the law already allows you to do without a licence, such as quotation for
criticism, review, research or reporting.


3B. SOURCE CODE AND CORES -- FOR CHECKING OUR RESULTS, NOT FOR OTHER FRONT ENDS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Project publishes results: test scores, comparisons with other
emulators, and the outcome of rebuilding its Cores from its own
documentation. The Source Code is provided so that those results can be
reviewed and substantiated: anyone who has it can check the Project's claims
for themselves.

For that purpose, and non-commercially, you may:

  (a) read and study the Source Code;
  (b) build the Cores from it, run and test them, and compare what you build
      with the built files the Project ships;
  (c) publish what you find, quoting reasonable excerpts of the Source Code
      where that is needed to show a finding, with credit as in section 3A.

Section 3 does not stop you doing anything this section permits.

PROHIBITED: you may not use the Source Code or the Cores, in part or in full,
to develop Other Front-End Software. That includes, among other things:

  * copying, translating, adapting or porting any of the Source Code into it;
  * running a Core, as Source Code or as built files, inside it or alongside
    it as part of it, or distributing a Core with it;
  * using the Source Code or a Core as the model, template or reference that
    it is written, built or tested against.

This restriction applies to the Cores in every form: their Source Code, and
their built files wherever they are published, including in the Project's
client and on its site. It is a prohibition, not a "please ask" item, and
section 7 does not apply to it.

This section takes nothing away from sections 2 and 3: you may still run the
Project, and change and share the WHOLE Project as they allow. It does not
limit section 3A, which covers the Research and Documentation, and it claims
no right over the facts about the original hardware.


3C. AUTOMATED USE -- NO AI-BUILT COPIES, NO AI TRAINING
-------------------------------------------------------
Without prior written permission, you may not:

  (a) use an Automated System to produce software (an emulator, an emulator
      core, a component of one, or Front-End Software) for which the
      Research and Documentation or the Source Code is the main source,
      whether you give the Automated System the text or code, in whole or
      in part, point it at them, or have it read them in any other way;
  (b) distribute, sell, publish or offer as a service any software produced
      as described in (a);
  (c) use the Research and Documentation or the Source Code, in whole or in
      part, to train, fine-tune or evaluate a machine-learning model, or put
      them in a dataset collected for that purpose;
  (d) remove or alter this licence, the NOTICE file, or any notice or
      identifying mark in the Project's files.

"The main source" means that the work supplies most of what the software
needs to know about the hardware it emulates, or that the output is a
substantially complete emulator or core, or a component of one, built from
it. For example:

  NOT PERMITTED: giving an Automated System an Emulation page, or the Source
  Code of a Core, and asking it to write the emulator or the part it
  describes; asking it to port or rewrite a Core in another language.

  PERMITTED: asking an Automated System to explain, search, translate or
  summarise a passage for your own understanding; using a fact you learned
  here, with credit, to have one fault fixed in a project you already have,
  as section 3A allows.

You may use ordinary tools, automated ones included, to read, search,
translate or summarise the Research and Documentation and the Source Code
for your own understanding, and you may use the facts you learn, with
credit, to improve your own existing projects as section 3A allows. Section
3B applies in addition to this section: what it prohibits stays prohibited,
whatever permission is given under this one.


3D. VERIFICATION -- REPEATING OUR CLEAN-ROOM PROOF
--------------------------------------------------
The Project publishes a clean-room method: an emulator is built from an
Emulation page alone, by a builder (which may be an Automated System) that
has not seen the Project's Source Code, and is then tested against the
Project's own Core. The Project's claims rest partly on that proof, so anyone
may repeat it.

You may follow that method, including with an Automated System, solely to
check the Project's published results, and only non-commercially. You may
publish your findings: the scores, the comparisons, the differences you
found and how you worked.

You may not distribute, publish, sell or offer as a service what you build
that way, or use it for anything other than that check. Sections 3A and 3C
do not stop you doing what this section permits, and this section permits
nothing else.


3E. TEXT AND DATA MINING -- RESERVED
------------------------------------
The rights holder expressly reserves the use of the Project, including the
Research and Documentation and the Source Code, for text and data mining,
within the meaning of Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790 on copyright
and related rights in the Digital Single Market, and of any similar law
elsewhere. This reservation is part of this licence, travels with every copy
of the Project, and applies wherever a copy is published.

No right of text and data mining is granted by this licence, except what
section 3D permits. This does not affect any exception that the law gives
and does not allow to be excluded, such as text and data mining for
scientific research under Article 3 of that Directive.


4. ATTRIBUTION
--------------
Any distribution of the Project, modified or unmodified, must:

  (a) name the CrystalWorlds.Media project visibly to the people who use it,
      not only in a source file comment;
  (b) link to the Project's canonical source where one is reachable;
  (c) retain this licence, the NOTICE file and the THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES file,
      unaltered;
  (d) state clearly that your version is modified, if it is, and not imply
      that this project produced, endorsed or approved it;
  (e) preserve the credits this project gives to others -- in particular the
      Game & Watch artwork credits on the About page and in
      THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES. Those name people who are not us, and dropping
      their credit is not yours to do.


4A. THE NAME -- NO PRETENDING TO BE US
--------------------------------------
"Crystal Worlds", "CrystalWorlds.Media" and the project's logo identify this
project and the people who make it. This licence grants no right to use them
except as this section allows.

  (a) A MODIFIED version of the Project must not be called "Crystal Worlds",
      "CrystalWorlds.Media" or any confusingly similar name, and must not use
      the project's logo. Before you distribute or host a modified version,
      replace the name and logo where they identify the site to its users
      (page titles, headings, the logo, the app name and icon).

  (b) No version, modified or unmodified, and no person or service hosting
      one, may present itself as this project, as run by this project, or as
      endorsed or approved by it. That includes using the name or logo in a
      domain name, an account or app-store name, or any promotion.

  (c) An UNMODIFIED copy may keep the name where it already appears in the
      Project's own pages, as long as (b) is respected.

  (d) The credit section 4 requires ("based on the Crystal Worlds project",
      with a link) is a permitted use of the name, and remains required.

Permission for any other use of the name or logo can be asked for under
section 7.


5. COMMERCIAL USE
-----------------
No commercial use of the Project Material is granted by this licence. That
includes selling it, selling anything that contains it, charging for access to
it, placing it behind advertising, and using it to promote a paid product or
service.

Commercial use requires prior written permission. Ask -- see section 7.

The one exception is quoting reasonable excerpts of the Research and
Documentation, with credit, which section 3A permits in commercial work too.


6. EXEMPT MATERIAL -- OTHER PEOPLE'S WORK, OTHER PEOPLE'S TERMS
---------------------------------------------------------------
Parts of this Project are not ours. Sections 2 to 5 DO NOT APPLY to them, and
nothing in this licence restricts, qualifies or adds conditions to the rights
their own licences grant you. Where this licence and an Exempt Material
licence differ, the Exempt Material licence governs that material.

This section is not a courtesy. The licences below forbid the imposition of
further restrictions, and this project intends to honour them exactly.

  (a) (Retired 2026-10-03.) This item covered the earlier Game & Watch
      engine, which is no longer part of the Project. The Game & Watch,
      Game Boy, Game Boy Color, Pokemon mini and NES cores are all
      clean-room work written here -- comprehension in, independent code
      out -- as recorded in each core's own provenance record. They are
      Project Material and sections 3 and 3B apply to them in full.

  (b) THE VIDEO PIPELINE -- LGPL-2.1-or-later and MPL-2.0.
      FFmpeg's libraries, compiled to WebAssembly by libav.js and shipped as
      the vendor/libav-* binaries, are under the GNU Lesser General Public
      License version 2.1 or later. The LGPL expressly requires that you be
      able to modify and replace that library, and permits commercial use;
      section 3 does not restrict it. The full licence text travels with the
      binaries at vendor/COPYING.LGPLv2.1, and the corresponding source is
      identified in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.

      The container muxing alongside it is mediabunny, under MPL-2.0,
      redistributed unmodified with its own header intact. MPL-2.0 permits
      commercial use and file-level modification; section 3 does not restrict
      it.

  (c) THE GAME & WATCH ARTWORK AND LAYOUT GEOMETRY.
      The community artwork and the 53 gnw_*.lay layouts are the work of
      hydef, DarthMarino, Henrik Algestam, ICEknight and others, released
      openly in a public forum thread. This project asserts no licence over
      their work on their behalf, and this licence grants you nothing in
      respect of it. What is owed there is credit, and it is given on the
      About page and in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.

  (d) ANY OTHER COMPONENT LISTED IN THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES, on the terms stated
      there.

THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES is the authoritative list. If it and this section ever
disagree, THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES governs.


7. ASKING -- EVERYTHING IS "PLEASE ASK"
---------------------------------------
Anything this licence does not grant, you may still have: ask. The one
exception is what section 3B prohibits, which is not granted on request.

That includes using the work commercially, using a Component in another
work, or anything else. Requests are welcomed and considered in good
faith, and non-commercial fan and preservation work is looked on kindly.
Permission must be in writing to count, and permission granted for one thing
is not permission for another.

HOW TO ASK

  Issues:  https://github.com/crystalworlds/client/issues
  Email:   info [at] crystalworlds.media

  (The address is written with "[at]" on purpose. The site assembles it at
  click time so that scrapers do not see a clean string in its HTML, and a
  plain-text licence in a public repository would defeat that. Replace the
  "[at]" with "@".)

An issue is preferred for anything that is not private: this licence requires
permission to be in writing, and a tracker provides that by default, with a
public record both sides can point at later.


